src/nist_sp_800_171/schema/*.yaml, src/nist_sp_800_171/datamodel/ and
project/ model Protecting Controlled Unclassified Information in
Nonfederal Systems and Organizations, which the National Institute of
Standards and Technology (NIST) publishes as Special Publication (SP)
800-171 Revision 3, https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-171r3 (May
2024), with the assessment procedures of SP 800-171A Revision 3,
https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-171Ar3, which NIST's catalog
carries. They are this project's rendering, written in its own words
with NIST as the source of the definitions; they are not NIST's, and
their use does not imply endorsement by NIST.

SP 800-171 states that it "may be used by nongovernmental organizations
on a voluntary basis and is not subject to copyright in the United
States. Attribution would, however, be appreciated by NIST." NIST's
statement on its works, read on 2026-10-29 at
https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications,
adds that such a work may be subject to copyright in other countries.

NIST's SP 800-171 catalog in OSCAL
----------------------------------

src/nist_sp_800_171/schema/oscal-content/ holds a copy of the catalog of
SP 800-171 Revision 3 that NIST publishes in the Open Security Controls
Assessment Language (OSCAL), retrieved from
https://github.com/usnistgov/oscal-content/tree/f0de6d8d8c059d374f15e0ae07508d82e370fc1d/nist.gov/SP800-171/rev3/json
(the v1.6.0 release), beside NIST's LICENSE.md from the root of the same
commit. Both files are identical to their counterparts there, and
scripts/check_upstream_release.py checks that they stay so. NIST's work
is in the public domain within the United States, and NIST dedicates it
to the worldwide public domain under CC0 1.0. The tests read the catalog
to check the schema against it; nothing in this repository changes it.

